      "id": 1,
      "text": "Preston Heights began as pioneer farmland in the 1830s, with settlers claiming prairie along Des Plaines and Hickory Creek near present‑day Joliet.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/preston_heights_illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The area’s low‑relief prairies and agricultural fields are shaped by its proximity to the Des Plaines River valley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/preston_heights_illinois",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Joliet’s 1834 canal terminus turned the surrounding region, including future Preston Heights, into a market gateway for farm produce heading east.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/preston_heights_illinois",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Most homes in Preston Heights were built between 1940 and 1969, and the neighborhood enjoys a 58.6% homeownership rate.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
